WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.480
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.680
Today we are going to have a look at the&nbsp;
state of egocentric videoconferencing. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:15.120
this doesn’t mean that only we get to speak during&nbsp;
a meeting, it means that we are wearing a camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.040
which looks like this, and the goal is to use&nbsp;
a learning algorithm to synthesize this frontal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:27.200
view of us. Now note that what you see here is&nbsp;
the recorded reference footage, this is reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.400
and this would need to be somehow synthesized&nbsp;
by the algorithm. If we could pull that off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.320
we could add a low-cost egocentric camera to smart&nbsp;
glasses, and it could pretend to see us from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.840
front, which would be amazing for hands-free&nbsp;
videoconferencing. That would be insanity.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.240
But wait a second. How is this even possible?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.520
For us to even have a fighting chance, there&nbsp;
are four major problems to overcome here.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.400
One, this camera lens is very close to&nbsp;
us, which means that it doesn’t see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.840
entirety of the face. That sounds extremely&nbsp;
challenging. And if that wasn’t bad enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.800
two, we also have tons of distortion&nbsp;
in the images, or in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.800
things don’t look like they look in reality,&nbsp;
we would have to account for that too. Three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.840
it would also have to take into account&nbsp;
our current expression, gaze, blinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:27.200
and more. Oh boy. And finally, four, the output&nbsp;
needs not be photorealistic, even better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.160
videorealistic. Remember, we don’t just need one&nbsp;
image, but a continuously moving video output.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.240
So the problem is, once&nbsp;
again, input, egocentric view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:44.080
output, synthesized frontal view. This is&nbsp;
the reference footage, reality if you will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.800
and now, let’s see how this learning-based&nbsp;
algorithm is able to reconstruct it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.200
Um…hello? Is this a mistake? They look&nbsp;
identical, as if they were just copied here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.960
No, you will see in a moment that it’s not a&nbsp;
mistake, this means that the AI is giving us a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:09.360
nearly perfect reconstruction of the remainder&nbsp;
of the human face. That is absolutely amazing.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.400
Now, it is still not perfect, there are some&nbsp;
differences. So how do we get a good feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:21.280
of where the inaccuracies are? The answer is a&nbsp;
difference image, look. Regions with warmer colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.080
indicate where the reconstruction is inaccurate&nbsp;
compared to the real reference footage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.000
For instance, with an earlier method by the name&nbsp;
pix2pix, the hair and the beard are doing fine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.840
while we have quite a bit of reconstruction&nbsp;
error on the remainder of the face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.960
So, did the new method do better than&nbsp;
this? Let’s have a look together. Oh yeah!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:48.080
It does much better across the entirety of&nbsp;
the face. It still has some trouble with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.960
cable and the glasses, but otherwise,&nbsp;
this is a clean, clean image. Bravo!

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.760
Now, we talked about the challenge of&nbsp;
reconstructing expressions correctly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.720
To be able to read the other person is of&nbsp;
utmost importance during a video conference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.280
So how good is it at gestures?

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.400
Well, let’s put it through an intense&nbsp;
stress test! Well, this is as intense as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:14.560
it gets without having access to Jim&nbsp;
Carrey as a test subject I suppose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.640
and I bet there was a lot of fun to be had in the&nbsp;
lab on this day. And the results are outstanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.440
especially if we compare it again&nbsp;
to the pix2pix technique from 2017.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:31.520
I love this idea, because if we can overcome&nbsp;
the huge shortcomings of the egocentric camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.520
in return, we get an excellent view of subtle&nbsp;
facial expressions and can deal with the tiniest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:43.840
eye movements, twitches, tongue movements,&nbsp;
and more. And it really shows in the results.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.880
Now please note that this technique needs to be&nbsp;
trained on each of these test subjects. About&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.800
four minutes of video footage is fine and this&nbsp;
calibration process only needs to be done once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.360
So, once again, the technique knows&nbsp;
these people and had seen them before.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:06.000
But in return, it can do even more. If all of this&nbsp;
is synthesized, we have a lot of control over this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:13.200
data and the AI understands what much of this data&nbsp;
means. So with all that extra knowledge, what else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.200
can we do with this footage? For instance, we can&nbsp;
not just reconstruct, but create arbitrary head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.800
movement. We can guess what the real head movement&nbsp;
is because we have a view of the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.400
we can simply remove it, or from the movement of&nbsp;
the background, we can infer what kind of head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:36.400
movement is taking place. And what’s even better,&nbsp;
we can not only get control over the head movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:44.240
and change it, but even remove the movement&nbsp;
from the footage altogether. And, we can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.920
remove the glasses and pretend to have dressed&nbsp;
properly for an occasion. How cool is that?

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.760
Now make no mistake, the paper contains a&nbsp;
ton of comparisons against a variety of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.880
works as well, here are some, but make sure to&nbsp;
check them all out in the video description.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.880
Now, of course, even this new method isn’t&nbsp;
perfect, for instance, it does not work all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:10.000
that well in low-light situations, but of course,&nbsp;
let’s leave something to improve for the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.200
paper down the line. And hopefully, in the near&nbsp;
future, we will be able to seamlessly get in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:20.960
contact with our loved ones through smart glasses&nbsp;
and egocentric cameras. What a time to be alive!

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.000
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

